{"id":"6abc26ba-afb2-472f-aeb4-8182da4e4135","object":"chat.completion","created":1785024746,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # validate\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or \\\"tokens\\\" not in case or \\\"calls\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case: missing tokens or calls\\\")\\n    tokens = case[\\\"tokens\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    if not isinstance(tokens, list) or not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"tokens and calls must be lists\\\")\\n    for call in calls:\\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"call must be dict\\\")\\n        for key in (\\\"call_id\\\",\\\"start\\\",\\\"end\\\",\\\"score\\\",\\\"replacement_tokens\\\"):\\n            if key not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"missing {key} in call\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(call[\\\"start\\\"], int) or not isinstance(call[\\\"end\\\"], int):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"start/end not int\\\")\\n        if call[\\\"start\\\"] >= call[\\\"end\\\"]:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"start >= end\\\")\\n        if call[\\\"start\\\"] < 0 or call[\\\"end\\\"] > len(tokens):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"span out of bounds\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(call[\\\"replacement_tokens\\\"], list):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"replacement_tokens not list\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(call[\\\"score\\\"], (int,float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"score not numeric\\\")\\n    # sort candidates\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\\\"score\\\"], c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"call_id\\\"]))\\n    selected = []\\n    for call in sorted_calls:\\n        overlap = False\\n        for sel in selected:\\n            if max(call[\\\"start\\\"], sel[\\\"start\\\"]) < min(call[\\\"end\\\"], sel[\\\"end\\\"]):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(call)\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c[\\\"start\\\"])\\n    selected_ids = [c[\\\"call_id\\\"] for c in selected]\\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)\\n    for call in reversed(selected):\\n        start = call[\\\"start\\\"]\\n        end = call[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        repl = call[\\\"replacement_tokens\\\"]\\n        result_tokens[start:end] = repl\\n    return {\\\"selected_call_ids\\\": selected_ids, \\\"tokens\\\": result_tokens}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that takes a payload (inner object) and returns a dictionary with keys \"selected_call_ids\" (list of strings) and \"tokens\" (list of strings). The task description: \"Apply accepted API calls to text while preserving registered span order, rejecting overlaps, and keeping all eligible calls.\" It mentions \"accepted_call_interleaving\". From the description:\n\n- Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\n- Greedily reject any span overlapping an already selected span.\n- Order accepted calls by source start for reporting.\n- Apply replacements from right to left so earlier offsets do not shift.\n- Return selected IDs and the exact interleaved token stream.\n\nSo the process:\n\nGiven a case object with:\n- \"tokens\": list of strings (original tokens)\n- \"calls\": list of objects each with \"call_id\", \"start\", \"end\", \"score\", \"replacement_tokens\".\n\nWe need to:\n1. Filter calls? \"all_eligible_calls\" - maybe all calls are eligible? No exclusion criteria given. So we consider all calls.\n2. Rank: descending score, then start (ascending?), then call_id (maybe lexicographic?). The description: \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\" Usually for ties: if scores equal, lower start first? But it says \"descending score, start position, then call ID.\" \"start position\" - likely ascending start (since lower start is earlier). Then call ID ascending. So we sort calls using a key: (-score, start, call_id). However, we need to confirm from the \"Order accepted calls by source start for reporting.\" That suggests after selection, order by start for reporting selected_call_ids.\n3. Greedy rejection: Iterate over sorted calls, and select if it does not overlap any already selected span. Overlap: if intervals [start, end) overlap (end exclusive? The examples: start and end indices are token indices. \"start\":1 \"end\":3 for call a indicates tokens at indices 1,2? Possibly half-open interval start inclusive, end exclusive. We need to confirm from fixture: tokens length 10, calls overlapping a (1-3), b (2-5), c (7-9). They overlap a and b share tokens 2? 1-3 includes indices 1,2; b includes 2,3,4? Actually 2-5 includes indices 2,3,4? Overlap on index 2. So we treat as half-open: [start, end). So check if intervals intersect: max(start1,start2) < min(end1,end2). Greedy select first non-overlapping.\n4. After selection, sort selected calls by their start position (ascending) for reporting. Then we need to produce the interleaved token stream: Apply replacements from right to left (i.e., process selected calls sorted by start descending) so earlier offsets don't shift. The \"replacement_tokens\" is a list of tokens to replace the span. So we take original tokens list, and for each selected call (in descending start order), replace the substring tokens[start:end] with replacement_tokens list. Then return the final tokens list.\n\nWe also need to return selected_call_ids in order of start (ascending). So we produce that.\n\nEdge cases: No calls selected -> selected_call_ids empty, tokens unchanged.\n\nCheck for validity: Validate every required field before computing a result. So we should check that case has \"tokens\" and \"calls\", and each call has \"call_id\", \"start\", \"end\", \"score\", \"replacement_tokens\". Raise ValueError if missing. Also check that start<end, indices within bounds? Might be required.\n\nImplementation details: Use only standard library.\n\nNow produce output: JSON with key \"implementation\" containing Python source. Must escape.\n\nWe'll write the function and then embed as string.\n\nLet's write solve(case):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or \"tokens\" not in case or \"calls\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: missing tokens or calls\")\n    tokens = case[\"tokens\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    if not isinstance(tokens, list) or not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"tokens and calls must be lists\")\n    # Validate each call\n    for call in calls:\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"call must be dict\")\n        for key in (\"call_id\",\"start\",\"end\",\"score\",\"replacement_tokens\"):\n            if key not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"missing {key} in call\")\n        if not isinstance(call[\"start\"], int) or not isinstance(call[\"end\"], int):\n            raise ValueError(\"start/end not int\")\n        if call[\"start\"] >= call[\"end\"]:\n            raise ValueError(\"start >= end\")\n        if call[\"start\"] < 0 or call[\"end\"] > len(tokens):\n            raise ValueError(\"span out of bounds\")\n        if not isinstance(call[\"replacement_tokens\"], list):\n            raise ValueError(\"replacement_tokens not list\")\n        if not isinstance(call[\"score\"], (int,float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"score not numeric\")\n    # Sort candidates: descending score, ascending start, ascending call_id\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\"score\"], c[\"start\"], c[\"call_id\"]))\n    selected = []\n    # Greedy selection\n    for call in sorted_calls:\n        overlap = False\n        for sel in selected:\n            if max(call[\"start\"], sel[\"start\"]) < min(call[\"end\"], sel[\"end\"]):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(call)\n    # Sort selected by start ascending\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c[\"start\"])\n    selected_ids = [c[\"call_id\"] for c in selected]\n    # Apply replacements right to left\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)  # copy\n    for call in reversed(selected):  # descending start\n        start = call[\"start\"]\n        end = call[\"end\"]\n        repl = call[\"replacement_tokens\"]\n        result_tokens[start:end] = repl\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_ids, \"tokens\": result_tokens}\n\nThat should work.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON output with the key \"implementation\". The entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with that key. So we'll generate a JSON string that contains the Python code as a string under \"implementation\".\n\nWe must be careful with escaping: newlines -> \\n, backslashes -> \\\\, quotes -> \\\". Since we are embedding the source code as a JSON string, we can use triple quotes in Python and then encode appropriately. We can write the code as a multiline string and then escape using json.dumps? But we are generating the output manually. We need to output a JSON object, not a Python object. But we can use Python's json module to serialize, but we are writing a response text. Since we are to output only the JSON, we can construct it carefully.\n\nSimplest: write the Python source as a string and then use json.dumps on a dict with key \"implementation\". But we are not in a Python environment? Actually we are generating the response text. We can write it manually.\n\nAlternatively, I can output using a Python script-like approach: But the response must be raw JSON. I'll manually write the JSON string with proper escaping.\n\nLet's write the code as a multiline string inside the JSON string using \\n for newlines and \\t for tabs, ensure quotes escaped.\n\nCode:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or \"tokens\" not in case or \"calls\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: missing tokens or calls\")\n    tokens = case[\"tokens\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    if not isinstance(tokens, list) or not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"tokens and calls must be lists\")\n    for call in calls:\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"call must be dict\")\n        for key in (\"call_id\",\"start\",\"end\",\"score\",\"replacement_tokens\"):\n            if key not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"missing {key} in call\")\n        if not isinstance(call[\"start\"], int) or not isinstance(call[\"end\"], int):\n            raise ValueError(\"start/end not int\")\n        if call[\"start\"] >= call[\"end\"]:\n            raise ValueError(\"start >= end\")\n        if call[\"start\"] < 0 or call[\"end\"] > len(tokens):\n            raise ValueError(\"span out of bounds\")\n        if not isinstance(call[\"replacement_tokens\"], list):\n            raise ValueError(\"replacement_tokens not list\")\n        if not isinstance(call[\"score\"], (int,float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"score not numeric\")\n    # sort candidates\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\"score\"], c[\"start\"], c[\"call_id\"]))\n    selected = []\n    for call in sorted_calls:\n        overlap = False\n        for sel in selected:\n            if max(call[\"start\"], sel[\"start\"]) < min(call[\"end\"], sel[\"end\"]):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(call)\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c[\"start\"])\n    selected_ids = [c[\"call_id\"] for c in selected]\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)\n    for call in reversed(selected):\n        start = call[\"start\"]\n        end = call[\"end\"]\n        repl = call[\"replacement_tokens\"]\n        result_tokens[start:end] = repl\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_ids, \"tokens\": result_tokens}\n\nNow we n